codex
`k-proof-technique` confirms the float operation can only be handled honestly as a supplied-primitive trust boundary: program-defined lookup/call/return must still execute, while float equality may remain opaque and the theorem must be conditional on its named contract. I’m isolating exactly that one pure primitive, with no state/control effects, and keeping integer arithmetic fully interpreted.
